"""
Helpers over Standard.File for copying, measuring, naming and removing files and trees.

- Nothing here follows a symbolic link: a link in a copied tree is copied as a link.
- A link in a measured tree counts nothing, and a link where a file is expected is refused.
- A copy never replaces what is at its target; it fails instead.
- A tree deeper than 64 directories is refused rather than walked.
- A failure is a Failure whose operation names the function and whose message names the path.
- Its kind is NotFound for a missing source, Invalid for a bad argument, else Unclassified.

    case copyDirectory("site", "site-backup") of
        when Right(right as done: Unit) do
            skip;
        when Left(left as failure: Failure) do
            let line: String := describeFailure(&failure);
            destroyFailure(failure);
    end case;
"""
pragma Targets(Names => "linux openbsd freebsd darwin");
import Standard.String (
    String
);
import Standard.Failure (
    Failure
);

module Standard.FileHelpers is
    """
    Copies the ordinary file at source to target, which must not exist,
    with the source's mode.  When the copy fails after target was made,
    target is removed.
    No contract: an Either result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    generic [R: Region, S: Region]
    function copyFile(source: Span[Nat8, R], target: Span[Nat8, S]): Either[Failure, Unit];

    """
    Copies the directory at source and everything in it to target, which
    must not exist: directories with their modes, ordinary files as
    copyFile copies them, and symbolic links as links.  A device, a
    socket or a fifo in the tree fails the copy, which then leaves what it
    had copied.
    No contract: an Either result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    generic [R: Region, S: Region]
    function copyDirectory(source: Span[Nat8, R], target: Span[Nat8, S]): Either[Failure, Unit];

    """
    The bytes of the ordinary files in the directory at path and in every
    directory below it.  Fails when a directory cannot be read, when the
    tree is deeper than 64 directories, or when the sizes add up to more
    than a Nat64 holds, which sparse files can claim.
    No contract: an Either result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function directorySize(path: Span[Nat8, R]): Either[Failure, Nat64];

    """
    A name for something new in directory, made from name with -1, -2 and
    so on: before the last dot of a file's name that is not its first
    byte, so report.txt becomes report-1.txt, and at the end of a
    directory's name or of a name without such a dot.  The first number
    whose name is free, up to 9999; None when all are taken or a name
    could not be looked at.
    No contract: an Option result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    generic [R: Region, S: Region]
    function unusedName(directory: Span[Nat8, R], name: Span[Nat8, S], isDirectory: Bool): Option[String];

    """
    Overwrites every byte of the ordinary file at path with random bytes
    from /dev/urandom, syncs it to the disk and removes it.  Right true
    when a file was removed, false when there was none.  On a disk that
    moves its writes, as a flash disk does, the old bytes may survive
    elsewhere; an encrypted disk is the answer there.
    No contract: an Either result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function secureDelete(path: Span[Nat8, R]): Either[Failure, Bool];

    """
    A size for people: the bytes as a number below 1024, else the size in
    K, M or G with one decimal, as 1.5K, rounded to the nearer tenth and
    on a tie to the even one, as Go's formatting does.
    No contract: a text whose length follows its input.
    """
    function formatSize(size: Nat64): String;
end module.
